From 105fafc4a1b68b7cd56d7bc8ed382987d3eb64a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 01:00:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 157/206] analyze_snapshot: retract the "walk differs at two
 sites" attribution (F68)

The F67 comment block read the two short parseNumber self_pp streams as
evidence that our subgraph walk diverges from theirs. That comparison had
two uncontrolled variables, not one: two analyze_snapshot binaries AND two
input snapshots (as.prog.ours.so and as.prog.theirs.so differ, 1928488 vs
1928496 bytes).

Controlling the input -- partition every function by whether its machine
code is byte-identical on both sides, sliced from each .so via
index_in_entries -> offset+size -- self_pp is ordered-equal on 776/776
byte-identical-code functions and not-ordered-equal on only 2 of the 3820
code-differing ones. Both offenders are ...parseNumber, which we emit 40
bytes shorter than they do; pool entry 1332 (+Infinity) is byte-identical
in both pools and we emit index 1332 elsewhere, so the decode is fine.

The shortfall is a gen_snapshot codegen gap, tracked as F68, not a
traversal gap in this file. Every number is reprintable with
spikes/shorebird_linkinfo_oracle/nc4_f68_codegen_pool_1332.sh.

Comment only; no code change, no rebuild.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
index 493810e0bce..e3f5a1029ea 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
@@ -1089,13 +1089,35 @@ static constexpr intptr_t kNoSelector = -1;
 // diff_units.sh shorebird went 41344 -> 39425 with the dedup off. Theirs plainly
 // does not deduplicate, so neither do we: the default is 0.
 //
-// ⚠️ This is NOT the clean reproduction F66 got on subgraph_selectors. Two prog
-// functions still come up one slot short -- index_in_entries 1580 and 1633,
-// ours 14 slots against their 15, both missing pool index 1332 -- so the
-// undeduped self_pp is ordered-equal on 4594 of 4596 functions and NOT on all
-// of them. The honest reading therefore stays "the values match and the order
-// matches everywhere we can see except at those two sites", not "our walk is
-// theirs". Those two functions are the whole remaining self_pp evidence debt.
+// ⚠️ Two prog functions still come up one slot short -- index_in_entries 1580
+// and 1633, ours 14 slots against their 15, both missing pool index 1332 -- so
+// the undeduped self_pp is ordered-equal on 4594 of 4596 functions. Those two
+// slots were first written up as "our WALK differs from theirs at two sites".
+// THAT ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG and is retracted here (F68, 2026-09-15).
+//
+// The ours/theirs comparison has two independent variables, not one: two
+// analyze_snapshot binaries AND two input snapshots. as.prog.ours.so and
+// as.prog.theirs.so are not the same file (1928488 vs 1928496 bytes), so a
+// per-function output difference can just as well be reporting that the
+// machine code being analysed differs. Controlling for that -- partition every
+// function by whether its bytes, sliced out of each .so via
+// index_in_entries -> offset+size, are byte-identical on both sides -- gives:
+//   byte-identical code: 776 functions, self_pp ORDERED-EQUAL on 776 of 776
+//   code differs:       3820 functions, not ordered-equal on 2
+// Both offenders are ...parseNumber, whose code we emit 40 bytes shorter than
+// they do (2628/2352 vs 2668/2392, sharing only a 32-byte prefix); pool entry
+// 1332 is byte-identical in both pools (+Infinity, 0x7ff0000000000000) and we
+// do emit index 1332 elsewhere (hello 2x, prog 1x), so the decode is fine --
+// that code simply does not contain the reference. The 44 subgraph_pp slots
+// are the same two depth-0 slots propagated to 44 callers.
+//
+// => The shortfall is a gen_snapshot CODEGEN difference in parseNumber, not an
+// analyze_snapshot traversal gap. Nothing in this file is implicated. Tracked
+// as F68. Reprint every number above with
+// spikes/shorebird_linkinfo_oracle/nc4_f68_codegen_pool_1332.sh (--negctl runs
+// the byte-flip control that proves the partition is read from the .so bytes).
+// F66's "values right, walk unproven" now applies only to code-differing
+// functions; on byte-identical code the walk is proven for self_pp.
 //
 // 1 = dedup ON (the historical behaviour). 0 = emit every pool reference the
 // classifier fires on, in walk order.
